% ============================================================================
% well-aquifer-type — which kind of aquifer each kind of well is drilled
% into (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? well_aquifer_type(artesian_well, $A)
%       % confined_aquifer
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `well_aquifer_type(well, aquifer_type)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the aquifer kind WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls.
%
% The BACKWARD query is the one a reader actually has: "which wells reach a
% confined aquifer?" returns both artesian kinds and not the water table
% well.
%
% THIRD USGS-sourced library, and the first from the Water Science School's
% aquifers page.
%
% Provenance: ONE sentence grounds all three rows.
%
%     "The illustration shows an artesian well and a flowing artesian well,
%      which are drilled into a confined aquifer, and a water table well,
%      which is drilled into an unconfined aquifer."
%
%     well                     aquifer_type
%     ----------------------   ------------------
%     artesian_well            confined_aquifer
%     flowing_artesian_well    confined_aquifer
%     water_table_well         unconfined_aquifer
%
% *** THAT SENTENCE IS A FIGURE CAPTION, NOT BODY PROSE, AND THIS HEADER
% SAYS SO. *** It is preceded on the page by "Media Sources/Usage: Public
% Domain. View Media Details", so a reader checking the citation should
% look under the illustration rather than scanning the article text. A
% citation that sends a reader hunting in the wrong part of a page is a
% worse citation, and the fix is to say where it lives.
%
% AND THE FIGURE CARRIES A THIRD-PARTY CREDIT. The caption block closes
% with "Credit: Environment and Climate Change Canada", so the
% illustration is republished by the USGS as Public Domain rather than
% drawn by it. The quoted text appears verbatim on a USGS page and that is
% what `locator` points at, but this header does NOT claim the USGS wrote
% the sentence, because that cannot be verified from the page. `trust
% authoritative` rests on the USGS being the first-party PUBLISHER of the
% material it serves, which is the claim actually supported.
%
% TWO WELLS, NOT ONE, ON THE CONFINED SIDE. The sentence names "an
% artesian well AND a flowing artesian well" as distinct things, so both
% are rows. Collapsing them into one atom would assert that the source
% treats them as the same well, which it does not.
%
% CONTEXT THE PAGE SUPPLIES BUT WHICH IS NOT A CITATION HERE. The article
% also says "Groundwater in aquifers between layers of poorly permeable
% rock, such as clay or shale, may be confined under pressure." That
% explains what CONFINED means, and it is genuinely useful to a reader —
% but it grounds no row of this relation, because it never says which
% wells reach which aquifer. Attaching it as a `cites` would dress up
% background as corroboration. A corroboration should support the rows, or
% it should not be in the envelope.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the USGS is a first-party .gov publisher. The
% page was CONTENT-verified by RAW TEXT EXTRACTION (HTTP 200, 285
% substantive sentences, zero soft-404 markers), never from a fetch
% summary, and the quoted sentence was confirmed to appear verbatim in
% that extraction.
%
% A NOTE ON AGREEMENT ACROSS PUBLISHERS, recorded because it is reassuring
% rather than because it is a row. This page says "The upper surface of
% this zone of saturation is called the water table", which is consistent
% with `zone-water-table-position.adj` — a table built from a National Park
% Service page — placing `zone_of_saturation` below the water table. Two
% independent .gov publishers using the same vocabulary the same way is
% worth knowing, but it is a definition rather than a new axis, so nothing
% here depends on it.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - DUG WELLS, DRILLED WELLS and DRIVEN WELLS get no rows. Those are
%     standard well types and a reader may well ask for them, but this
%     sentence names three wells and those are not among them. Answering
%     from general hydrology knowledge is exactly what a grounded recall
%     library must not do.
%   - `spring` is not a well and gets no row.
%   - The relation says which aquifer a well is DRILLED INTO. It does not
%     say how deep, how much water, or whether the well flows without
%     pumping — the last of which the page discusses for artesian wells but
%     which is a different relation.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table well_aquifer_type {
    columns well, aquifer_type

    row (artesian_well, confined_aquifer)
    row (flowing_artesian_well, confined_aquifer)
    row (water_table_well, unconfined_aquifer)

    source "The illustration shows an artesian well and a flowing artesian well, which are drilled into a confined aquifer, and a water table well, which is drilled into an unconfined aquifer."
    locator "https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/aquifers-and-groundwater"
    trust authoritative
}
